CARDS = [
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "What is a process, and how is it different from a thread?", "A process is a program in execution plus everything the kernel keeps for it: an address space, open file descriptors, credentials, signal state, a scheduling record. A thread is one path of execution within that — it has its own registers, stack and thread ID, and shares everything else. That is the whole difference, and it explains both why threads communicate in nanoseconds and why they can corrupt each other."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "What actually happens when a process makes a system call?", "A single instruction switches the processor into kernel mode and jumps to one fixed address the kernel registered at boot. The program controls when, not where. The kernel saves the registers, switches to a kernel stack, looks up the call number in a table, validates any user pointers, does the work, and returns. About 50 to 100 nanoseconds, which is why everything batches."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "Explain the Node.js event loop.", "It is a scheduler in user space. It calls <code>epoll_wait</code>, which blocks until any of the sockets it is watching has data, and then runs a callback for each ready one. libuv structures that into phases — timers, poll, check, close — with the <code>nextTick</code> and microtask queues drained after every callback. The key property is that it is cooperative: it has no way to interrupt a running callback, which is why one slow synchronous function stalls every request in the process."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "Why is Node good at handling many concurrent connections?", "Because it replaces a context switch with a function call. Thread-per-connection costs about 100 KB and a full context switch per event — several microseconds with cold caches. One thread handling ten thousand connections costs one stack, learns about all ready sockets in one call, and switches between them for free. Three orders of magnitude. It says nothing about being faster at computation, which is the honest boundary."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "Is Node single-threaded?", "Your JavaScript is. The process is not — it has four libuv threadpool threads by default for file I/O, DNS, crypto and zlib, plus V8's compiler and garbage collection threads. So a Node process can exceed 100% CPU in <code>top</code>. What it cannot do is run two of your functions at once."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "What is virtual memory and why does it exist?", "Every process gets its own address space, and hardware translates each address through a per-process page table before touching physical memory. It exists because the alternative — giving each process a contiguous physical region — fragments memory, caps a process at physical RAM, and makes sharing impossible. Paging fixes all three: any page can map to any frame, permissions are checked per page, and two processes can share a frame."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "What is a page fault?", "The hardware tried to translate an address and the page table said the page is not present, or the access is not permitted. It traps into the kernel, which decides what it means: load the page from disk, allocate and zero a fresh one, do a copy-on-write copy, grow the stack, or — if the address is genuinely not part of the process — deliver SIGSEGV. Six very different things, one hardware event."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "What is a container, really?", "An ordinary Linux process. It is started with its own namespaces so it sees a different process table, mount tree and network stack; put in a cgroup that caps its CPU and memory; given a root filesystem assembled from stacked read-only image layers with one writable layer on top; run as a specific user with most capabilities dropped and a syscall filter installed. There is no container object in the kernel — it is a way of calling <code>clone()</code>."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "How is a container different from a virtual machine?", "A VM has its own kernel and boots; a container shares the host kernel and is just a process, so it starts in milliseconds instead of seconds and costs a process instead of a kernel's worth of memory. The trade is the isolation boundary: a VM's is enforced by hardware, a container's rests entirely on kernel correctness, so a kernel vulnerability is a shared vulnerability. In practice you use both — containers inside VMs."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "What is the difference between a Kubernetes CPU request and a limit?", "A request becomes a cgroup weight — a relative share that only matters when the node is contended, and it is also what the scheduler places on. A limit becomes a hard quota: at most N milliseconds of CPU per 100 ms period, after which every thread in the container is stopped until the next period, even if the machine is idle. So a request guarantees you something and a limit can only slow you down."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "What does OOMKilled mean, and what is exit code 137?", "The container exceeded its cgroup memory limit and the kernel sent SIGKILL. 137 is 128 plus 9, and 9 is SIGKILL — the shell convention for a signal death. Because it is SIGKILL, nothing runs: no graceful shutdown, no flush, no log line. And it is not necessarily a leak — page cache from the container's own file writes counts against the limit too."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "Walk me through what happens when I type a URL and press enter.", "DNS resolution, then a TCP handshake and a TLS handshake — one to three round trips before any data. The request goes out; on the server side the NIC DMAs the packet into memory and raises an interrupt, a softirq walks it up the stack into a socket buffer, that marks a descriptor ready in epoll, the server process wakes, reads, parses, and runs the handler. The response comes back the same way, and the browser parses, styles, lays out, paints and composites it. Every one of those steps is a place latency can hide."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "What is the difference between blocking and non-blocking I/O?", "A blocking read puts the thread to sleep until data arrives — it costs zero CPU, but that thread can do nothing else, so many clients need many threads. A non-blocking read returns immediately with EAGAIN if there is nothing, which is only useful combined with something like epoll that tells you which of thousands of sockets are ready. That combination is what lets one thread serve ten thousand connections."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "What is a file descriptor?", "An index into a per-process table. Each entry points at a kernel object — a file, a socket, a pipe, a terminal — so the same small integer means completely different things in different processes. Descriptors 0, 1 and 2 are stdin, stdout and stderr by convention, and because <code>open</code> always returns the lowest free number, shell redirection is implemented entirely by rearranging that table between fork and exec."),
("near_universal", "Near-universal", "What is a deadlock and how do you prevent one?", "Two parties each holding something the other needs, in a cycle, with no way to take anything back. Coffman's four conditions are mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait — you need all four, so breaking any one prevents it. In practice you break circular wait with lock ordering: define a total order over your locks and always acquire in it. In database code, that means sorting the row IDs before locking them."),
]
